# Line endings. The kit is written on Linux and now ships files that Windows itself reads,
# so the working tree is pinned here rather than left to whatever `core.autocrlf` is set to
# on the machine doing the checkout. Without this file a clone on Windows converts every
# text file to CRLF -- which breaks `worksafe/cosmic/install.sh` under WSL or git-bash with
# `\r: command not found` -- and a commit from Windows would quietly normalise the .reg and
# .theme files to LF, which is the one thing they must not be.

# Everything is LF in the working tree, on every platform. This is what the repository
# already holds, so it renormalises nothing.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Except the Windows surface. A .reg or a .theme is read by Windows, and install.cmd is run
# by cmd.exe, which needs CRLF -- a batch file with LF endings mis-parses `goto` labels and
# multi-line blocks. These three keep CRLF in the working tree on every platform, which is
# also what `build/windows.py` compares the generated files against: the checker reads them
# as bytes, so an LF checkout would fail it rather than ship silently.
elevated/windows/*.reg    text eol=crlf
elevated/windows/*.theme  text eol=crlf
elevated/windows/*.cmd    text eol=crlf
